# Implement theme-safe decorative titles

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical evidence is domain structure, not an operating raster or PDF
renderer; the finite design acceptance is explicitly bounded.

Support layered lettering, outlines, highlights, and motifs in digital mode and
derive a sober, single-pen alternative without losing title hierarchy.

Current executable evidence now freezes a caller-produced digital/live title
pair around one stable semantic title identity and one exact hierarchy value.
The digital treatment structurally carries layered lettering, outline,
highlight,
and motif inputs while the live treatment remains caller-owned one-pen intent.
Validation rejects title-identity drift first and hierarchy drift second while
allowing the mode-specific treatments themselves to differ. Successful
comparison now returns constructor-sealed evidence borrowing the exact plan.

A 256-case compact oracle crosses 64 hierarchy values with both identity- and
hierarchy-drift bits while varying digital/live treatment values independently
and requires direct/sealed admission parity.

Title-style derivation, hierarchy semantics, typography, colors, motif
generation, one-pen geometry, layout, rendering, and conversion orchestration
remain open.

## Finite theme-safe title treatments and single-pen fallback

**Inputs:** one accepted semantic title identity with exact hierarchy level,
selected light/dark theme, digital palette, optional motif assets and requested
Digital/PDF or single-pen Live output. **Outputs:** a bounded decorative
proposal in digital modes or a sober single-pen title path preserving identity
and hierarchy; unsupported resources refuse instead of silently disappearing.

**One title example:** semantic title `t1` reads `Biología`, level 1. The
light Digital treatment uses dark-blue lettering, one pale-gold underline,
a thin outline and a small leaf motif outside the text's bounding box. The
dark treatment uses light lettering and a contrast-checked warm accent; both
retain identical text, semantic owner and level-1 spatial hierarchy. Motifs
are independently owned decorative objects, not a rewrite of title text.

**Single-pen alternative:** for a one-pen Live plan, retain the selected
writer's `Title` role at declared 5 mm cap height, add one simple underline
with the same pen if safe, and omit color-dependent outlines/highlights,
shadow and leaf fill only after an explicit capability conversion receipt.
The derived title still reads `Biología`, retains `t1` and level 1, and does
not claim a new handwriting profile or automatic pen changes.

**Theme and geometry policy:** require minimum legible foreground/background
contrast for both palettes before rendering, and keep line/outline separation
inside the writable page region. Motifs cannot overlap the source title,
clip its accents or degrade small annotation readability. The conversion
preview lists every omitted or substituted effect; no unmapped motif becomes
an invisible live stroke or a hidden false-success Export.

**Refusal example:** a dark theme makes the gold underline unreadable, the
motif spills into the writable margin, the selected handwriting profile lacks
`Title` glyphs, or Live asks for multi-color layering. Preserve source text
and reject the affected treatment or request a different explicit style.
Missing text glyphs cannot be masked by decorative image lettering.

**Assumptions and limits:** the repository can structurally compare a
caller-produced digital/live title pair for identical title identity and
hierarchy. Palette computation, decorative layout, theme contrasts, actual
handwriting synthesis, alternate geometry and rendering are absent. This is
one finite title design, not a general theme engine or generated Live plan.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for title/hierarchy pairing; theoretical for theme,
  decorative and one-pen vector output, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** one light/dark digital style, same-hierarchy Live fallback,
  explicit omitted effects and contrast/glyph refusal.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** decorative paths, contrast
  engine, genuine single-pen alternative or renderer integration.
